 ### [Back-to-Front Compositing](2078-back-to-front-compositing.md)
-Back-to-Front Compositing is a volume rendering technique that iteratively accumulates color and opacity along a viewing ray by processing samples from the farthest intersection with the volume to the nearest. It solves the problem of blending semi-transparent layers into a final image by applying the Over operator, which is the mathematical inverse of the Under operator used in front-to-back rendering. Unlike front-to-back compositing, this method does not require tracking accumulated opacity for the color calculation, but it does not support early ray termination.
+Back-to-Front Compositing is a volume rendering technique that determines the final pixel color by accumulating samples along a viewing ray from the far boundary (back) to the camera (front). It solves the problem of blending semi-transparent layers to produce a coherent image while enabling hardware alpha blending implementation where objects approach the viewer. Unlike its sibling [Front-to-Back Compositing (Over Operator)](3360-front-to-back-compositing-over-operator.md), which tracks accumulated opacity to enable early ray termination, back-to-front compositing does not require tracking accumulated opacity for the color calculation but consequently lacks early termination support.
 
