 ## Background
-Alpha compositing ray casting originates from the volume rendering integral formulated in light transport theory [S3, unofficial]. The method was popularized by Drebin, Carpenter, and Hu in their 1988 paper "Volume Rendering" [S4, unofficial], which introduced the use of pre-multiplied color-opacity values and the OVER operator for efficient compositing. The technique is modeled after optical film recorders, simulating the projection of light through a gel-like substance [S6, unofficial]. It has become the standard compositing method for direct volume rendering, forming the basis for many modern GPU-based renderers and differentiable rendering pipelines (e.g., NeRF, 3D Gaussian Splatting) [S9, unofficial][S14, unofficial][S23, unofficial].
+The method integrates samples with optical properties along viewing rays, following the volume rendering integral from light transport theory [S3, unofficial]. The technique was popularized by Drebin, Carpenter, and Hu in their 1988 paper "Volume Rendering" [S4, unofficial], which introduced the use of pre-multiplied color-opacity values and the OVER operator for efficient compositing. The method is modeled after optical film recorders, simulating the projection of light through a gel-like substance [S6, unofficial]. The compositing rule is identical to that used by volumetric radiance fields (NeRF) and 3D Gaussian Splatting [S9, unofficial][S23, unofficial], and is implemented in differentiable rendering pipelines [S14, unofficial].
 
